            <ArticleTitle>Long-lasting analgesic effect of radiofrequency treatment of the lumbosacral dorsal root ganglion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>227-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECT" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The authors conducted a study to establish the benefit of radiofrequency (RF) treatment of the lumbosacral dorsal root ganglion (DRG) as a therapy to reduce symptomatic pain in patients with chronic spinal pain radiating to the leg.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two hundred seventy-nine patients were evaluated after undergoing their first RF treatment of the DRG. A four-point pain perception scale was used. Short-term effect was documented after 2 months. The influence of surgical history on outcome was examined by using chi-square analysis. The mean duration of analgesic effect was calculated by applying a probit survival analysis. Two months after undergoing RF treatment, 59% of patients reported satisfactory pain reduction. No serious adverse effects were noted. Surgical history was shown to have no significant effect on outcome. The long-term half-life time of pain reduction was 44.5 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of RF in the treatment of DRG appears to be a useful and safe therapy in patients with chronic spinal pain that radiates to the leg. The initial success rate is approximately 60%. It seems to lead to a time-limited effect on the target structure, and the mean duration of pain reduction is approximately 3.7 years. The mechanism of action remains unclear.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
